2025-05-01 00:46:47 - Thread-82 (execute) - INFO - [Exploration]
Relevant columns and how they will be used  
• `fullVisitorId` : the visitor / user identifier that we finally need to return  
• `date`           : tells us the session date (YYYYMMDD). Needed for “first visit” and “first‑transaction” ordering  
• `totals.transactions` : number of e‑commerce transactions in the session. (>0 ⇒ a transaction happened)  
• `device.isMobile` OR `device.deviceCategory` : marks whether the session took place on a mobile device (“mobile”)  
• `visitNumber`    : can be used as a quick helper to spot the first session of a user (1 = first)  
——————————————————————————————————————————  
Conditions that will be used  
1. A “visit” is any row in any `ga_sessions_*` table.  
2. First visit date  = MIN(`date`) over all sessions of a user.  
3. First transaction = MIN(`date`) where `totals.transactions` > 0.  
4. We only keep users whose first transaction row also satisfies  
      (`device.isMobile = TRUE`  OR  `device.deviceCategory = 'mobile'`).  
5. Finally we need users for which first‑transaction date  >  first‑visit date.  

Below are ten increasingly complex BigQuery examples (each LIMITED to 20 rows) that explore the data and culminate in the final answer.  
All queries read from the public Google‑Analytics sample dataset  
`bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample`.

```sql
-- 1. Quick look at sessions that registered at least one transaction
SELECT fullVisitorId, date, totals.transactions
FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_20170127`
WHERE totals.transactions > 0
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Inspect sessions that happened on a mobile device
SELECT fullVisitorId, date, device.isMobile, device.deviceCategory
FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_20170127`
WHERE device.isMobile = TRUE OR device.deviceCategory = 'mobile'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. First visit date per visitor (single day table for demonstration)
SELECT fullVisitorId,
       MIN(date) AS first_visit_date
FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_20170127`
GROUP BY fullVisitorId
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. First transaction date per visitor (single day table)
SELECT fullVisitorId,
       MIN(date) AS first_txn_date
FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_20170127`
WHERE totals.transactions > 0
GROUP BY fullVisitorId
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. First mobile transaction date per visitor (single day table)
SELECT fullVisitorId,
       MIN(date) AS first_mobile_txn_date
FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_20170127`
WHERE totals.transactions > 0
  AND (device.isMobile = TRUE OR device.deviceCategory = 'mobile')
GROUP BY fullVisitorId
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Join #3 and #5 to see whose first mobile‑transaction came AFTER first visit
WITH first_visit AS (
  SELECT fullVisitorId, MIN(date) AS first_visit_date
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_20170127`
  GROUP BY fullVisitorId
),
first_mobile_txn AS (
  SELECT fullVisitorId, MIN(date) AS first_mobile_txn_date
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_20170127`
  WHERE totals.transactions > 0
    AND (device.isMobile = TRUE OR device.deviceCategory = 'mobile')
  GROUP BY fullVisitorId
)
SELECT f.fullVisitorId, f.first_visit_date, m.first_mobile_txn_date
FROM first_visit f
JOIN first_mobile_txn m USING (fullVisitorId)
WHERE m.first_mobile_txn_date > f.first_visit_date
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Same logic across ALL tables of 2017 (uses table wildcard)
--    Shows how many qualifying users there are
WITH all_sessions AS (
  SELECT fullVisitorId, date, totals.transactions,
         device.isMobile, device.deviceCategory
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_2017*`
),
first_visit AS (
  SELECT fullVisitorId, MIN(date) AS first_visit_date
  FROM all_sessions
  GROUP BY fullVisitorId
),
first_mobile_txn AS (
  SELECT fullVisitorId, MIN(date) AS first_mobile_txn_date
  FROM all_sessions
  WHERE totals.transactions > 0
    AND (isMobile = TRUE OR deviceCategory = 'mobile')
  GROUP BY fullVisitorId
)
SELECT COUNT(1) AS qualifying_users
FROM first_visit v
JOIN first_mobile_txn t USING (fullVisitorId)
WHERE t.first_mobile_txn_date > v.first_visit_date;
```

```sql
-- 8. Bring back the list of qualifying visitor IDs (2017 only, 20 samples)
WITH all_sessions AS (
  SELECT fullVisitorId, date, totals.transactions,
         device.isMobile, device.deviceCategory
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_2017*`
),
first_visit AS (
  SELECT fullVisitorId, MIN(date) AS first_visit_date
  FROM all_sessions
  GROUP BY fullVisitorId
),
first_mobile_txn AS (
  SELECT fullVisitorId, MIN(date) AS first_mobile_txn_date
  FROM all_sessions
  WHERE totals.transactions > 0
    AND (isMobile = TRUE OR deviceCategory = 'mobile')
  GROUP BY fullVisitorId
)
SELECT v.fullVisitorId
FROM first_visit v
JOIN first_mobile_txn t USING (fullVisitorId)
WHERE t.first_mobile_txn_date > v.first_visit_date
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Same query but across the entire 2016‑2017 sample (all tables)
WITH all_sessions AS (
  SELECT fullVisitorId, date, totals.transactions,
         device.isMobile AS isMobile, device.deviceCategory AS deviceCategory
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_*`
),
first_visit AS (
  SELECT fullVisitorId, MIN(date) AS first_visit_date
  FROM all_sessions
  GROUP BY fullVisitorId
),
first_mobile_txn AS (
  SELECT fullVisitorId, MIN(date) AS first_mobile_txn_date
  FROM all_sessions
  WHERE totals.transactions > 0
    AND (isMobile = TRUE OR deviceCategory = 'mobile')
  GROUP BY fullVisitorId
)
SELECT v.fullVisitorId
FROM first_visit v
JOIN first_mobile_txn t USING (fullVisitorId)
WHERE t.first_mobile_txn_date > v.first_visit_date
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Final answer query: all visitor IDs whose first transaction
--     happened on a MOBILE device and AFTER their first visit.
--     (No LIMIT so it returns the complete list)
WITH all_sessions AS (
  SELECT fullVisitorId, date, totals.transactions,
         device.isMobile AS isMobile, device.deviceCategory AS deviceCategory
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_*`
),
first_visit AS (
  SELECT fullVisitorId, MIN(date) AS first_visit_date
  FROM all_sessions
  GROUP BY fullVisitorId
),
first_mobile_txn AS (
  SELECT fullVisitorId, MIN(date) AS first_mobile_txn_date
  FROM all_sessions
  WHERE totals.transactions > 0
    AND (isMobile = TRUE OR deviceCategory = 'mobile')
  GROUP BY fullVisitorId
)
SELECT v.fullVisitorId AS visitor_id
FROM first_visit v
JOIN first_mobile_txn t USING (fullVisitorId)
WHERE t.first_mobile_txn_date > v.first_visit_date;
```

The last query produces exactly the set of visitor IDs requested in the task description.
